The proof consists of two main steps. The first step establishes a property of the underlying degree sequence, which states that the sum of the degrees of `small' number of vertices is `small'.  egin{lemma} Under Assumption~ef{assumption1}.ef{assumption1-2}, given any $\varepsilon, r >0$, there exists $\delta = \delta(\varepsilon, r) >0$, such that for all sufficiently large $n$, the sum of degrees of the $r$-neighborhood of \emph{any} $\delta n$ vertices is at most~$\varepsilon n$, i.e. $\sum_{u\in\mathcal{N}[U,r]}d_i<\varepsilon n$ uniformly over all subsets $U\subseteq [n]$ such that $|U|< \delta n$. \end{lemma} In the second step we show that $r$ can be chosen large enough, so that with high probability, the total number of vertices at depth more than $r$ in all hanging trees combined, is arbitrarily `small'. This is formalized in Lemma~ef{lem:small-hanging-trees}.  egin{lemma} For any $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $r=r(\varepsilon)>0$ such that with high probability $$\left|\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}\setminus\mathcal{N}[\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}^2,r]ight|<\varepsilon n.$$ \end{lemma} 
oindent Given Lemmas~ef{lem:small-nbd} and~ef{lem:small-hanging-trees}, the proof of Lemma~ef{lem:thin-tree} can now be completed. Consider the following equivalent re-statement of Lemma~ef{lem:thin-tree}:  egin{quotation} 
oindent For any $\varepsilon,  eta>0$, there exists $\delta = \delta(\varepsilon)>0$ and $n_0= n_0(\varepsilon,  eta)$, for which, the probability that there exists a subset $\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathcal{T}_h$ with $|\mathcal{T}|<\delta n$ and $| igcup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\{T\}|\geq \varepsilon n$, is at most $ eta$ for all $n\geq n_0$. \end{quotation} To show the above statement, fix any $\varepsilon,  eta>0$. Using Lemma~ef{lem:small-hanging-trees}, choose $r=r(\varepsilon/2)$ and $n_1 = n_1(\varepsilon/2, eta)$, such that for all $n\geq n_1$, $$\prob{\left|\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}\setminus\mathcal{N}[\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}^2,r]ight|\geq rac{\varepsilon n}{2}}< eta.$$ Also, appealing to Lemma~ef{lem:small-nbd}, we choose $\delta = \delta(\varepsilon/2, r)$ and $n_2 = n_2(\varepsilon,r)$, such that for all $n\geq n_2$, $
exists\ U\subseteq V$ with $|U|< \delta n$ and $\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}[U,r]}d_i\geq \varepsilon n/2$. Now observe that if there exists a subset $\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathcal{T}_h$ with $|\mathcal{T}|<\delta n$ and $| igcup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\{T\}|\geq \varepsilon n$, then $$	ext{either}\quad igg| igcup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathcal{N}[w_T,r] igg|> rac{\varepsilon n}{2} \quad 	ext{or}\quad  igg| igcup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\{T\}\setminus  igcup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathcal{N}[w_T,r] igg|> rac{\varepsilon n}{2},$$ where $w_T$ is the unique vertex in $T$ that has a neighboring vertex in $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}^2$. Choose $n_0 = \max\{n_1,n_2\}$ so that, for all $n\geq n_0$, the probability of the first event is 0, and that of the latter event is at most $ eta$, which concludes the proof.

It is crucial to observe that for $t(\varepsilon) = \hat{n} - \varepsilon \ell_n$, the graph $\mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon))$ is distributed as a configuration model with the criticality parameter  egin{equation} 
u_n(\varepsilon) = rac{\sum_{i\in [n]}d_i(d_i-1)-2\varepsilon \ell_n}{\sum_{i\in [n]}d_i} =
u_n -2\varepsilon, \quad 	ext{and} \quad \lim_{n	o\infty}
