First note that it is enough to show  egin{equation} \prob{\ensuremath{\mathrm{CM}_n( oldsymbol{d})} 	ext{ is bipartite} \vert \mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon))}\leq \ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-C_0 n(1+\varepsilon_n)}, \end{equation} for some $\varepsilon_n \geq 0$ almost surely. Recall Algorithm~ef{algo-CM}. Also, observe that if $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t(\varepsilon))$ is non-bipartite, then $\ensuremath{\mathrm{CM}_n( oldsymbol{d})}$ will also be non-bipartite. Indeed, if $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t(\varepsilon))$ is non-bipartite, then it must contain an odd cycle of black nodes, and the process of merging degree one (red) vertices does not affect these existing cycles. Thus if there is an odd-length cycle in $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t(\varepsilon))$, that cycle will be present in $\ensuremath{\mathrm{CM}_n( oldsymbol{d})}$ as well. So assume $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t(\varepsilon))$ is bipartite. For $t\geq t(\varepsilon)$ we will now describe an algorithm for partitioning $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t)$ into two vertex-disjoint sets $H_1(t)$ and $H_2(t)$ in a coupled way. The sets $H_1(t)$ and $H_2(t)$ are such that if $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t)$ is bipartite, then these are the unique partite sets. For $i=1,2$, let $H_i^B(t)$ and $H_i^R(t)$ be the set of black and red vertices in $H_i(t)$, respectively. Also, let $R(t)$ and $R_P(t)$ denote the set of all red vertices and red pairs in $\mathcal{G}_n(t)$, respectively (recall that a pair is two degree one vertices joined with each other). With a little abuse of notation, we will also write $H_i^B(t)$, $R(t)$ etc.~to denote the cardinality of the respective sets.  egin{algo} 
ormalfont Initially consider the unique bipartition of $\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t(\varepsilon))$: $$\mathscr{C}_{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}(t(\varepsilon)) = H_1(t(\varepsilon))\sqcup H_2(t(\varepsilon)),\ \mathrm{say}.$$ At time step $t> t(\varepsilon)$, suppose two red vertices $v_1$ and $v_1$ are coalesced to form a new black vertex $v$ of degree 2. Then the sets $H_1(t)$ and $H_2(t)$ are updated according to the following rule:  egin{enumerate}[{
ormalfont (i)}] \item If both $v_1, v_2\in H_i^R(t-1)$ for either $i=1$ or 2, then $$H_i(t)=  ig(H_i(t-1)\setminus \{v_1,v_2\} ig)\cup \{v\},$$ and the other partition remains unchanged. \item If $v_1\in H_1^R(t-1)$ and $v_2\in R(t-1)\setminus (H_2^R(t-1)\cup H_1^R(t-1))$, then  egin{align*} H_1(t) &= \Big(H_1(t-1)\setminus \{v_1\}\Big) \cup\{v\} igcup_{k=1}^\infty \Big(\mathcal{N}[v_2,2k]\setminus \mathcal{N}[v_2,2k-1]\Big),\ H_2(t) &= H_2(t-1) igcup_{k=0}^\infty \Big(\mathcal{N}[v_2,2k+1]\setminus \mathcal{N}[v_2,2k]\Big) \end{align*} where by convention, the zero neighborhood of a vertex is the vertex itself. \item If $v_1\in H_2^R(t-1)$ and $v_2\in R(t-1)\setminus (H_2^R(t-1)\cup H_1^R(t-1))$, then repeat Step (ii) above by interchanging the role of $H_1$ and $H_2$. \item If both $v_1, v_2\in R(t-1)\setminus (H_2^R(t-1)\cup H_1^R(t-1))$, then $H_i(t) = H_i(t-1)$, $i=1,2$. \item If $v_1\in H_1^R(t-1)$ and $ v_2\in H_2^R(t-1)$ or vice versa, then remove $v_1$ and $v_2$ from their respective sets, and add $v$ to $H_1(t)$ or $H_2(t)$ arbitrarily. \end{enumerate} \end{algo} \vspace{.25cm} 
oindent Note that for $t_1<t_2$, $H_1^B(t_1)\subseteq H_1^B(t_2)$ and $H_2^B(t_1)\subseteq H_2^B(t_2)$. Also, if $\ensuremath{\mathrm{CM}_n( oldsymbol{d})}$ is bipartite, then $\mathcal{G}_n(t)$ must be bipartite for all $t>t(\varepsilon)$. In that case, Case (v) of Algorithm~ef{algo:partition} should not occur, otherwise the bipartiteness will be lost. We now claim that the number of red vertices in both partitions will become order $n$ at some time step after $t(\varepsilon)$. Afterwards we establish that if the claim is true, then the probability that Case (v) of Algorithm~ef{algo:partition} will not occur is exponentially small. As a result, $\ensuremath{\mathrm{CM}_n( oldsymbol{d})}$ is bipartite with exponentially small probability.  egin{claim} For sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $\delta_1=\delta_1(\varepsilon)>0$ and $\delta_2=\delta_2(\varepsilon)>0$ such that  egin{equation} \prob{ orall\ t>t(\varepsilon),\ H_1^R(t)<\delta_1n\ \mbox{m or }H_2^R(t)<\delta_2n\vert\mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon))} \leq \ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-C_0n (1+\ensuremath{o_{\scriptscriptstyle\ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}}}(1))}. \end{equation} \end{claim}  egin{claimproof} Note that since $\mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon))$ is distributed as a configuration model, \cite[Theorem~2.3~(i)]{JL09} and Lemma~ef{lem:num-pairs} respectively yields  egin{equation}  rac{H_1^R(t(\varepsilon))+H_2^R(t(\varepsilon))}{n} \ensuremath{\xrightarrow{\mathbbm{P}}} r(\varepsilon)>0\qquad\mathrm{and}\qquad  rac{R_P(t(\varepsilon))}{n}\ensuremath{\xrightarrow{\mathbbm{P}}} r_P(\varepsilon)>0. \end{equation} Due to~\eqref{eq:total-red-giant}, one of the sets $H_1^R(t(\varepsilon))$ or $H_2^R(t(\varepsilon))$ has atleast $r(\varepsilon) n/4$ red vertices with high probability. Without loss of generality, let the part be $H_1^R(t(\varepsilon))$. Since at each time step $t> t(\varepsilon)$ only degree 2 vertices are created, the set of red vertices can deplete by at most 2. Therefore, for $t^*(\varepsilon) = t(\varepsilon)+\min\{r(\varepsilon) n/8,\varepsilon\ell_n/2\}$,  egin{equation} \inf_{t(\varepsilon)\leq t\leq t^*(\varepsilon)} H^R_1(t)\geq rac{r(\varepsilon)n}{8}=:\delta_1n. \end{equation} It is important to note that both $r(\varepsilon)$ and $r_P(\varepsilon)$ are bounded away from zero as $\varepsilon	o 0$. Thus $\delta_1$ remains positive even when $\varepsilon$ is chosen small enough. Recall from Algorithm~ef{algo:partition}(ii) that during the time interval $[t(\varepsilon), t^*(\varepsilon)]$, $H_2^R$ increases by at least one if some red vertex in $H_1^R$ is coalesced with one of the vertices outside the set $H_1^R\cup H_2^R$, in particular, with one belonging to some red pair in $R_P$. Using \eqref{eq:delta1up}, at each time step the probability of the latter event, conditionally on $\mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon))$, is atleast $$ rac{r(\varepsilon)n}{8}r_P(\varepsilon)n{n_1 + 2 n_2\choose 2}^{-1}\geq \delta_1 c_1(1+\ensuremath{o_{\scriptscriptstyle\ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}}}(1)),$$ for some $c_1\in (0,1]$. Denote by $\mathcal{A}(t)$ the cumulative number of red vertices thus added to $H_2^R$ up to time $t$ starting from $t(\varepsilon)$. Observe that $\mathcal{A}(t)$ stochastically dominates a binomial random variable with $\min\{r(\varepsilon) n/8,\varepsilon\ell_n/2\}$ number of trials and the success probability atleast $\delta_1 c_1(1+\ensuremath{o_{\scriptscriptstyle\ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}}}(1))$. Therefore, standard concentration inequalities for the binomial distribution \cite[Corollary 2.3]{JLR00} yields  egin{equation} \ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}(\mathcal{A}(t^*(\varepsilon))\leq \delta_2' n|\mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon)))\leq \ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-C_0'n (1+\ensuremath{o_{\scriptscriptstyle\ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}}}(1))} \end{equation} for some suitable $\delta_2'>0$ and a constant $C_0'>0$. Further notice that some of the red vertices in $H_2^R(t)$ have been coalesced to form new black vertices during $[t(\varepsilon),t^*(\varepsilon)]$. This can occur if only if at the coalescence step both red vertices are selected from $H_2^R$, which occurs with probability at most ${H_2^R(t)\choose 2}/{n_1\choose 2}$. Denote the cumulative number of such coalesced red vertices up to time $t$ by $\mathcal{B}(t)$. Define $$	au(\varepsilon):= \inf\{t\geq t(\varepsilon): H_2^R(t) \geq \delta_2'n/2\}\wedge t^*(\varepsilon).$$ Then observe that for $t\in [t(\varepsilon), 	au(\varepsilon)]$, the quantity $\mathcal{B}(t)$ is dominated by a binomial random variable with $\min\{r(\varepsilon) n/8,\varepsilon\ell_n/2\}$ number of trials and success probability at most $\delta_2' n/2n_1$. The mean of this binomial random variable is of the order at most $\varepsilon\delta_2'n$, which can be made arbitrarily small compared to $\delta_2'n$ by choosing $\varepsilon$ small enough. Therefore, standard concentration inequalities for the binomial distribution again imply that  egin{equation} \prob{\mathcal{B}(	au(\varepsilon))\geq \delta_2'n/4\ \vert\ \mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon)), \{H_1^R(t) \leq \delta_2'n, orall\ t(\varepsilon) \leq t < 	au(\varepsilon)\}}\leq \ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-C_0''n(1+\ensuremath{o_{\scriptscriptstyle\ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}}}(1))} \end{equation} for some constant $C_0''>0$. Now set $\delta_1$ as above and $\delta_2 := \delta_2'/2$, and observe that $H_2^R(t) = H_2^R(t(\varepsilon))+\mathcal{A}(t)-2\mathcal{B}(t)$. Thus \eqref{eq:bound-deg-1-A} and \eqref{eq:bound-deg-1-B} yields that either the probability that the following will not occur is exponentially small  egin{align*} H_2^R(	au(\varepsilon)) &= H_2^R(t(\varepsilon))+\mathcal{A}(	au(\varepsilon))-2\mathcal{B}(	au(\varepsilon))\geq \delta_2 n, \end{align*} or $H_2^R(t)>\delta_2n$ for some $t\in[t(\varepsilon), 	au(\varepsilon)]$. In either of the two cases, this completes the proof of Claim~ef{claim:good-cut}. \end{claimproof} 
oindent Let $t^{**}(\varepsilon):=	au(\varepsilon)+(\delta_1\wedge\delta_2)n/4$. Observe that due to Claim~ef{claim:good-cut} and the argument given above~\eqref{eq:delta1up},  egin{equation} \inf_{	au(\varepsilon)\leq t\leq t^{**}(\varepsilon)} H^R_i(t)\geq  rac{\delta_in}{4}\qquad i = 1,2. \end{equation} Recall that while forming the degree 2 vertices from $\mathcal{G}_n(	au(\varepsilon))$ to $\ensuremath{\mathrm{CM}_n( oldsymbol{d})}$, the bipartiteness is lost if one red vertex is chosen from~$H_1^R$ and the other is chosen from~$H_2^R$. Therefore, for any $t\in[	au(\varepsilon), t^{**}(\varepsilon)]$,  egin{align*} &\prob{	ext{Bipartiteness is not preserved at time }t \vert \mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon)),\ H_1^R(	au(\varepsilon))\geq \delta_1n,\ H_2^R(	au(\varepsilon))\geq \delta_2n}\ &\geq  rac{\delta_1\delta_2n^2}{16{n_1 \choose 2}} = (1-c_0)(1+\ensuremath{o_{\scriptscriptstyle\ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}}}(1)), \end{align*} for some $c_0\in (0,1)$. Thus the probability that bipartiteness is preserved troughtout the time interval $[	au(\varepsilon), t^{**}(\varepsilon)]$ given $\mathcal{G}_n(t(\varepsilon))$, $H_1^R(	au(\varepsilon))\geq \delta_1n$, and $H_2^R(	au(\varepsilon))\geq \delta_2n$ is upper bounded by $\exp(-C_0(1+\ensuremath{o_{\scriptscriptstyle\ensuremath{\mathbbm{P}}}}(1))n)$ for some constant $C_0>0$. The proof of Lemma~ef{lem:prob-bipartite} is now complete.
